WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.450
Our far-field sensor could never

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:03.450
reach the school office.

3
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.300
Its radio reaches two hundred metres;

4
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.640
the office is six hundred away.

5
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.740
So we didn't buy a bigger radio.

6
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.920
We asked its neighbours to

7
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.000
pass the message on.

8
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.760
Today: mesh networks, where devices pass

9
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.700
messages along to get them home.

10
00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.050
Most networks you know are a

11
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.510
star: every device talks straight

12
00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.450
to one hub, like a router.

13
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.380
That's simple, but every device

14
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.400
must be in range of the hub.

15
00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.330
Anything farther away is cut off.

16
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.950
In a mesh network, devices pass

17
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.190
messages for each other.

18
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.860
A sensor sends its reading to a

19
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.060
neighbour in range, who passes it on.

20
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.470
Each step is called a hop.

21
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.040
Hop by hop, the message

22
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.100
reaches the gateway.

23
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.260
It's like football.

24
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.100
Nobody kicks the ball from one

25
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.700
end straight into the goal.

26
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.790
The team passes it, player to player.

27
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:04.950
And if one player is marked,

28
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.590
the ball goes another way.

29
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:08.210
Quick check!

30
00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.410
A goat knocks over a sensor in

31
00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:12.370
the middle of the field.

32
00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.710
What happens to messages from

33
00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.250
the sensors behind it?

34
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.220
They take another route, through

35
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.520
neighbours that still work.

36
00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:22.990
It may cost an extra hop,

37
00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:24.310
but they get through.

38
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.120
That's why a mesh keeps working

39
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.600
when one device fails.

40
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.300
But a mesh still has weak points.

41
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.740
A sensor with only one

42
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.600
neighbour hangs by a thread.

43
00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.770
A gap wider than the radio's range

44
00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.630
splits the network in two.

45
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.280
And if the gateway fails,

46
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:45.860
nothing gets out at all.

47
00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.970
The fix is often cheap: a relay, a simple

48
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.490
radio that only passes messages on.

49
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.950
Put one in a gap, and the two halves join.

50
00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.230
Give lonely sensors a second neighbour.

51
00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.650
And keep the gateway powered.

52
00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.720
But every hop costs battery on the sensor

53
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.620
that relays it, and adds a little delay.

54
00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.790
So sensors next to the gateway work

55
00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.410
hardest: they carry everyone's messages.

56
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:17.300
Your turn.

57
00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.270
Zainab's sensor by the river has

58
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.530
only one neighbour, and floods keep

59
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:23.810
knocking that neighbour over.

60
00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:25.410
What's the best fix?

61
00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.650
Add a relay on higher ground: a second

62
00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.270
neighbour means a second route.

63
00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.410
Remember: messages hop from neighbour

64
00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.190
to neighbour to the gateway.

65
00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.290
When one device fails,

66
00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.930
they find another way.

67
00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.750
Fill gaps with relays, and

68
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:43.730
look after the gateway.

69
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.460
Try it tomorrow: a paper mesh.

70
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.220
Draw ten sensors and a gateway on a map.

71
00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.450
Cut a string as long as the radio's range,

72
00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.090
and link every two sensors it reaches.

73
00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.790
Find one that's cut off, and

74
00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.850
add a relay to rescue it.
